  "note": "The accents the interface offers. 'voice' is the espeak voice, verified to exist; 'rule' means the core rewrites a standard transcription into this accent, which is what covers the words no dictionary lists; 'words' is how many words this accent has a reading of its own for, which arrive as a pack. An accent can have both, and the good ones do: the pack answers the words it holds and the rule answers everything else, which is most of a vocabulary. The cascade keeps them apart, so a word the pack spelled out is never shifted again. 'default' is the accent a language is read in until the reader picks another: there is always one, since a reading in no accent in particular is a mix of them. 'tags' are the labels Wiktionary gives this accent's pronunciations, best first, which tools/build_accent_overlays.py reads its words from. 'heard' are the further labels Wiktionary puts on a recording's speaker in this accent (\"a=Southern England\"), which with the tags, the short name and the file's own region (en-us-word.ogg) pick the recording a reader who chose this accent hears.",
